(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-physiology-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Approximately how genetically identical are humans at the genome level, according to this lecture?","choices":["100% identical with absolutely no variation across the entire genome","99.8% identical at the genome level, 99.999% identical in coding regions","50% identical across the entire coding and non-coding genome","75% identical across the entire coding and non-coding genome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Humans are approximately 99.8% identical at the genome level and 99.999% identical in the coding regions, despite having an estimated 25,000-30,000 genes. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What are the three phases of interphase (which makes up more than 95% of the cell life cycle)?","choices":["Prophase, metaphase, anaphase (phases of mitosis itself)","Mitosis, cytokinesis, and apoptosis (the cell death phases)","Transcription, translation, and replication (the molecular phases)","G1 phase, S phase (DNA synthesis), and G2 phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Interphase (>95% of the cell cycle) consists of the G1 phase, S phase (DNA synthesis), and G2 phase, distinct from the M phase (mitosis and cytokinesis). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What forms on the lagging strand during DNA replication (S phase)?","choices":["Only continuous new DNA with no fragments","Okazaki fragments","Ribosomal subunits","Clathrin-coated pits"],"answer":1,"exp":"During S phase, DNA replication involves formation of Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand, with \"new\" DNA proofread by DNA polymerase and gaps filled by DNA ligase. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What is the role of DNA ligase in DNA repair?","choices":["It unwinds the DNA double helix ahead of the replication fork during synthesis","It replaces mismatches (cut out by DNA polymerase during proofreading) with complementary nucleotides","It forms new ribosomal subunits within the nucleolus during synthesis","It transcribes new mRNA strands directly from the DNA template"],"answer":1,"exp":"After DNA polymerase \"proofreads\" new DNA and cuts out mismatches, DNA ligase replaces the mismatches with complementary nucleotides; a mistake during transcription resulting in an abnormal protein is called a mutation. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is the basis for cell differentiation, according to this lecture?","choices":["Permanent physical loss of unneeded genes as cells proliferate and specialize over time","Selective repression/expression of specific genes (not loss of genes), often via one part of the body inducing effects on another","A simple increase in mitochondrial number alone, as cells mature and specialize","Purely random mutation accumulating within the genome across many successive cell divisions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cell differentiation results not from the loss of genes but from the selective repression/expression of specific genes, with development occurring in large part through \"inductions\" — one part of the body affecting another. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which tissues are described as having rapid rates of cell growth/turnover?","choices":["Cardiac muscle and neurons only","Neurons and striated muscle","Bone marrow, skin, and intestinal epithelia","None — all tissues grow at the same rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bone marrow, skin, and intestinal epithelia show rapid cell growth, while smooth muscle, neurons, and striated muscle grow slowly or never; growth factors, contact inhibition, and negative-feedback cellular secretions determine growth rate. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"What does the lipid bilayer act as, functionally?","choices":["A barrier to water and water-soluble substances","A repository of genetic information for the cell","The primary site of ATP synthesis in the cell","A pump that requires ATP for every type of transport"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lipid bilayer acts as a barrier to water and to substances that dissolve in water, though specific molecules can still cross via membrane proteins. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Roughly how many different proteins involved in transport do cell membranes contain?","choices":["Fewer than 10","Over 10,000","Over 300","About 50"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cell membranes contain over 300 different proteins that play a role in transport, providing specificity and function (ion channels, carrier proteins). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the key difference between diffusion and active transport across the cell membrane?","choices":["Diffusion requires direct ATP hydrolysis to proceed; active transport occurs spontaneously down its gradient without energy","Active transport only occurs during the mitotic phase of the cell cycle, never during interphase","Diffusion occurs down a concentration gradient with no additional energy required; active transport occurs against a gradient and requires ATP","They are simply two different names describing the exact same passive transport process across membranes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Diffusion occurs down a concentration gradient and requires no additional energy, while active transport occurs against a concentration gradient and requires energy (ATP), using a protein carrier. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What distinguishes facilitated diffusion from simple diffusion?","choices":["There is no meaningful difference between the two; both terms simply describe passive movement of any dissolved molecule down its concentration gradient without any protein involvement","Simple diffusion only occurs in muscle cells, where lipid-soluble hormones cross the sarcolemma directly without any channel or carrier proteins","Facilitated diffusion requires ATP directly, hydrolyzing one molecule of ATP for each solute molecule transported across the membrane","Facilitated diffusion involves water-soluble molecules crossing via protein channels/transport proteins, while simple diffusion is for lipid-soluble molecules crossing directly through the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Simple diffusion allows lipid-soluble molecules to move readily across the membrane based on lipid solubility, while facilitated diffusion allows water-soluble molecules to cross via protein channels and other transport proteins. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What do aquaglyceroporins transport, in addition to water?","choices":["Glycerol, urea, and ammonia","Only calcium and magnesium ions","Only glucose and fructose molecules","Only sodium and potassium ions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aquaglyceroporins are a subset of aquaporins that transport glycerol, urea, and ammonia across cell membranes, in addition to water. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What are the two main types of ion channels?","choices":["Aquaporins and aquaglyceroporins, both selective for water and small solutes","Primary active transporters and secondary active transporters, both requiring ATP","Ungated channels and gated channels (voltage-gated or chemical/ligand-gated)","Symporters and antiporters, both classified as secondary active transport carriers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ion channels are either ungated (transport determined by size, shape, and charge) or gated — either voltage-gated (e.g., voltage-gated Na+ channels) or chemical/ligand-gated (e.g., nicotinic acetylcholine receptor channels). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What terminates the end-plate potential at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["Reuptake of intact ACh into the presynaptic terminal","Closure of voltage-gated Ca2+ channels alone, with no ACh breakdown","Simple diffusion of ACh away from the synaptic cleft only","Acetylcholinesterase (AChE), which breaks down ACh"],"answer":3,"exp":"Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) breaks down ACh in the synaptic cleft, terminating the end-plate potential and allowing the neuromuscular junction to reset for the next signal. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is a ligand?","choices":["Only an endogenous substance made naturally by the body itself","A specialized subtype of voltage-gated ion channel","A structural scaffolding protein found within muscle","Any substance that binds to a receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"A ligand is any substance that binds to a receptor; acetylcholine and norepinephrine are considered \"physiological\" ligands (provided by the body), and receptors are often classified/named by reference to their ligand. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What distinguishes nicotinic (nACh) from muscarinic (mACh) acetylcholine receptors?","choices":["Muscarinic receptors are found only within skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction, while nicotinic receptors are restricted entirely to smooth muscle organs","Nicotinic receptors are stimulated by nicotine; muscarinic receptors are stimulated by muscarine — both are subtypes of ACh receptor named by their distinguishing ligand","Nicotinic receptors do not respond to acetylcholine at all, being activated exclusively by nicotine itself, an entirely separate and unrelated ligand","They are functionally identical G protein-coupled receptors, differing only in the name given by the pharmacologist who first described them"],"answer":1,"exp":"ACh receptors are subdivided into nicotinic (nACh, stimulated by nicotine) and muscarinic (mACh, stimulated by muscarine) receptors, illustrating how receptors are often classified by reference to distinguishing ligands. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Approximately how many acetylcholine molecules are contained in a single synaptic vesicle at the neuromuscular junction?","choices":["~1,000,000","~100","~10,000","~10"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each synaptic vesicle at the neuromuscular junction contains approximately 10,000 molecules of ACh, with roughly 300,000 vesicles formed per nerve terminal. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How is information encoded by a series of action potentials, given that their amplitude is constant?","choices":["By the frequency of action potentials","Information cannot be encoded by APs","By reversing the direction of propagation","By varying the AP amplitude"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because action potential amplitude is constant (they do not summate), information is encoded by the frequency of action potentials rather than by amplitude. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the typical threshold voltage for initiating an action potential, relative to resting potential?","choices":["About 100 mV negative to resting potential","About 15 mV positive to resting potential","About 50 mV positive to resting potential","Exactly equal to resting potential"],"answer":1,"exp":"Action potentials are typically initiated once the membrane depolarizes to about 15 mV positive to the resting potential (the threshold voltage), triggering the all-or-none upstroke. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the function of T-tubules in excitation-contraction coupling?","choices":["They are the site of actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling, forming the contractile apparatus that shortens the sarcomere during contraction","They store Ca2+ long-term in the same manner as the sarcoplasmic reticulum, using calsequestrin to bind and buffer intracellular calcium","They synthesize ATP for contraction via oxidative phosphorylation, functioning as an extension of the mitochondrial network within the fiber","They are invaginations of the sarcolemma, filled with extracellular fluid, that transmit action potentials deep into the muscle fiber"],"answer":3,"exp":"T-tubules are invaginations of the sarcolemma filled with extracellular fluid that penetrate, branch, and form networks within the muscle fiber, transmitting action potentials deep into the fiber to trigger Ca2+ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What structures make up the \"triad\" in skeletal muscle excitation-contraction coupling?","choices":["Two T-tubules flanking a single terminal cisterna of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, forming the site of DHP-ryanodine receptor coupling","Actin, myosin, and titin filaments interdigitating within a single sarcomere to generate contractile force","Three myofibrils bundled together by a shared connective tissue sheath called the endomysium","The junction of two terminal cisternae of the SR with one T-tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The \"triad\" is the junction between two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum and a T-tubule, where the DHP receptor (voltage sensor on the T-tubule) communicates with the ryanodine receptor (Ca2+ release channel on the SR). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the key difference between skeletal and cardiac muscle excitation-contraction coupling?","choices":["Skeletal muscle uses Voltage-Activated Calcium Release (VACR) via the DHP receptor; cardiac muscle uses Calcium-Activated Calcium Release (CACR), where Ca2+ entry through an L-type channel triggers further SR Ca2+ release","Both muscle types rely on direct mechanical coupling between the DHP receptor and ryanodine receptor, with no extracellular calcium influx required to trigger SR release in either tissue type","Neither muscle type uses the ryanodine receptor for SR calcium release; both rely instead on direct calcium diffusion through gap junctions between adjacent sarcomeres","Skeletal muscle uses Calcium-Activated Calcium Release (CACR), where extracellular Ca2+ entry triggers further release; cardiac muscle uses Voltage-Activated Calcium Release (VACR) sensed directly by the DHP receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"In skeletal muscle, the trigger for SR Ca2+ release is voltage (Voltage-Activated Calcium Release, sensed by the T-tubule DHP receptor); in cardiac muscle, the trigger is Ca2+ itself (Calcium-Activated Calcium Release), where Ca2+ entering via a voltage-gated L-type channel triggers further Ca2+ release from the SR via the ryanodine receptor. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What protein does Ca2+ bind to within the sarcoplasmic reticulum to facilitate its storage after being pumped back in?","choices":["Troponin","Titin","Calsequestrin","Calmodulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"After a muscle contraction, Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR by a Ca2+ pump, where it binds to calsequestrin to facilitate storage, terminating the contraction. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the sarcomere?","choices":["A supporting type of glial cell found in muscle","The smallest contractile unit of a muscle fiber","The connective sheath surrounding a muscle fiber","The junction between a T-tubule and two cisternae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sarcomere is the smallest contractile unit of a muscle fiber, composed of interdigitating thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the function of titin (connectin) in the sarcomere?","choices":["It binds Ca2+ directly to trigger contraction, functioning as a backup calcium sensor alongside troponin C on the thin filament complex","It hydrolyzes ATP to power the cross-bridge cycle, functioning as an accessory motor protein alongside the myosin head","It forms the structural backbone of the thin filament, providing the scaffold onto which actin monomers polymerize","It acts as a molecular spring providing passive elasticity, prevents overstretching, and stabilizes thick filaments centered between actin filaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Titin is a large, springy filamentous protein spanning half the sarcomere (Z disc to M band) that acts as a molecular spring preventing overstretching, provides passive elasticity, stabilizes the thick myosin filaments centered between actin filaments, and contributes to active tension generation. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What kinds of long-term changes can neuropeptides cause at synapses?","choices":["Only transient changes in synaptic delay lasting a few milliseconds, with no lasting structural effect on the synapse","No lasting changes — they act only transiently and are metabolized within milliseconds like small molecule transmitters","Changes in the number of neuron receptors, long-term opening/closure of ion channels, and changes in the number and size of synapses","Immediate, brief ion channel opening only, lasting a few milliseconds before the channel closes and resets completely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Neuropeptides cause long-term changes including altered numbers of neuron receptors, long-term opening or closure of ion channels, and changes in the number and size of synapses; they act on metabotropic receptors and are often co-released with small molecule transmitters. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which category of somatic sensation includes static position sense and rate of change (proprioception)?","choices":["Nociceptive (specifically fast pain)","A fourth, unnamed sensory category","Mechanoreceptive (specifically proprioceptive)","Thermoreceptive (specifically cold)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatic sensations are classified as mechanoreceptive (including tactile — touch, pressure, vibration, tickle, itch — and proprioceptive — static position, rate of change), thermoreceptive (heat/cold), and nociceptive (pain/tissue damage). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What type of nerve fiber carries signals from unmyelinated free nerve endings that detect pain, temperature, and itch?","choices":["Type A-beta fibers","Type C fibers","Type A-alpha fibers","Only myelinated fibers can detect pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unmyelinated free nerve endings that detect pain, temperature, and itch use slowly adapting type C fibers, while myelinated free nerve endings (pain, temperature) use type A-delta fibers. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the four classes of sensory nerve fibers mentioned that transmit receptor information to the brain?","choices":["Alpha, beta, gamma, delta","A, B, C, D","Fast, medium, slow, very slow","Type I, II, III, IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sensory nerve fibers are classified as alpha, beta, gamma, and delta types, transmitting receptor information to the brain at different conduction velocities. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the relationship between stimulus strength and receptor potential allow a wide response range?","choices":["Receptor potential rises linearly with stimulus strength, so discrimination ability is identical at every intensity level","Small changes in stimulus strength can be discerned at low intensity, while only large changes can be discerned at high intensity","Large changes can be discerned at low intensity, while only small changes can be discerned at high intensity","Only small changes can ever be discerned, regardless of baseline intensity, due to a fixed receptor threshold"],"answer":1,"exp":"Small changes in stimulus strength can be discerned when baseline intensity is low, while only large changes can be discerned when intensity is already high — this logarithmic-like relationship allows receptors to function over a wide range of stimulus intensities. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are the three broad divisions of the nervous system described in the overview?","choices":["Cortical division, subcortical division, and spinal division","Somatic division, visceral division, and enteric division","Sensory division, integrative division, and motor division","Central division, peripheral division, and autonomic division"],"answer":2,"exp":"The nervous system is organized into a sensory division (tactile, visual, auditory, olfactory), an integrative division (processing information, creating memory), and a motor division (responding to and moving within the environment). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What type of sensory information does the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system transmit, and what is its key structural feature?","choices":["Only crude touch and itch sensations, via small unmyelinated C fibers with very low spatial fidelity","Touch, vibration, position, and fine pressure, via large myelinated A-beta fibers with high spatial fidelity, decussating in the medulla","Pain and temperature, via slow unmyelinated C fibers with poor spatial fidelity, decussating in the spinal cord","Only pain and crude touch, via a three-neuron chain that decussates immediately at the level of entry"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dorsal column-medial lemniscal system contains large myelinated A-beta fibers (30-110 m/sec) that transmit touch, vibration, position, and fine pressure rapidly, with high spatial fidelity maintained throughout the tract, and decussates in the medulla oblongata. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How does the anterolateral system differ from the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system?","choices":["It uses smaller myelinated (A-delta) and unmyelinated (C) fibers for slower transmission, decussates in the spinal cord, has low spatial orientation, and transmits a broad spectrum including pain, temperature, crude touch, and itch","It uses large myelinated A-beta fibers for rapid transmission (30-110 m/sec), decussates in the medulla, maintains high spatial fidelity, and transmits only touch, vibration, and position sense","It only transmits fine touch and vibration, using large myelinated fibers that decussate late in the medulla, thereby preserving precise spatial localization throughout the entire pathway","It never decussates, remaining entirely ipsilateral throughout its course, and transmits proprioceptive information exclusively to the cerebellum via the spinocerebellar tracts"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterolateral system uses smaller myelinated (A-delta) and unmyelinated (C) fibers for slower transmission (0.5-40 m/sec), decussates in the spinal cord, has a low degree of spatial orientation, and transmits a broad spectrum of modalities including pain, thermal sensations, crude touch/pressure, tickle, and itch. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What clinical uses do dermatomes have?","choices":["Only relevant to the motor system, used exclusively to localize corticospinal tract lesions in the brainstem","Localizing spinal cord lesions and explaining rash patterns from viruses like varicella zoster that hibernate in ganglia","No clinical significance beyond textbook anatomy diagrams used in first-year histology courses","Only used to diagnose peripheral vascular disease by mapping regions of poor arterial perfusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"A dermatome is the area of skin supplied by sensory neurons from a single spinal nerve ganglion; dermatomes are clinically useful for localizing cord lesions and explain why viruses like varicella zoster (hibernating in ganglia) cause rash in an associated dermatome (shingles). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is notable about the representation of different body parts in the somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus)?","choices":["The hands have the greatest area of cortical representation, followed by the lips and tongue, while the trunk has the least","The trunk has the greatest representation, followed by the thigh and lower leg, while the fingers have minimal representation","The lips have the greatest area of cortical representation, followed by the face and thumb, while the trunk and lower body have the least","All body parts have equal representation except the genitals, which show a small area of disproportionate cortical mapping"],"answer":2,"exp":"The somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus) shows unequal representation of the body: the lips have the greatest area of representation, followed by the face and thumb, while the trunk and lower body have the least — illustrated by the sensory homunculus. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What happens when the somatosensory association area (Brodmann areas 5 and 7) is lost?","choices":["Only motor deficits occur, specifically spastic paralysis of the contralateral limbs and face","No functional deficit occurs, since the primary somatosensory cortex fully compensates for the loss","Complete loss of all sensation on the contralateral side, including pain, temperature, and touch","Inability to recognize complex objects, and neglect of (or refusal to acknowledge) the contralateral body/world"],"answer":3,"exp":"Loss of the somatosensory association area (Brodmann areas 5 and 7 in the parietal lobe, which deciphers complex sensory associations) causes an inability to recognize complex objects and can cause neglect of, or even refusal to acknowledge ownership of, the contralateral body. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is lateral inhibition, and what does it improve?","choices":["The capacity of an excited neuron to reduce activity of neighboring neurons, improving contrast and two-point discrimination","The capacity of an excited neuron to increase activity of neighboring neurons, blurring contrast and reducing two-point discrimination","A mechanism restricted to motor pathways that recruits additional neurons to amplify a weak descending signal","The convergence of multiple presynaptic terminals onto one neuron, allowing spatial summation of subthreshold inputs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lateral inhibition is the capacity of an excited neuron to reduce the activity of neighboring neurons, improving the degree of contrast and thus improving two-point discrimination; it occurs at every synaptic level of the dorsal column system. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What sign, seen during a reflex test like the patellar reflex, suggests cerebellar disease?","choices":["A delayed reflex onset only","Complete absence of the reflex","An unusually strong single kick with no oscillation","Multiple oscillations of the leg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Multiple oscillations of the leg during a reflex test (like the patellar reflex) is a sign of cerebellar disease, reflecting impaired damping of the motor response. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What would happen to the stretch reflex response if only the extrafusal muscle contracted without the intrafusal (spindle) fibers contracting?","choices":["The stretch response would become exaggerated due to unopposed gamma motor neuron drive","The spindle would fire continuously at its maximum discharge rate throughout the contraction","The spindle would go slack (unloaded), making the stretch response unavailable","The extrafusal fibers would fatigue rapidly and the reflex arc would be permanently disabled"],"answer":2,"exp":"If the extrafusal muscle contracts without corresponding contraction of the intrafusal fiber ends (i.e., without alpha-gamma co-activation), the spindle goes slack and the stretch response becomes temporarily unavailable — this is why co-activation is necessary during voluntary movement. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is proprioception?","choices":["The detection of chemical changes in the blood that regulate breathing rate","The sensing of the relative position of body parts and the strength of effort used in movement","The perception of two-point discrimination and fine touch localization on the skin","The sense of body orientation relative to gravity, mediated by the vestibular apparatus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Proprioception (from Latin proprius, \"one's own,\" and perception) is the sensing of the relative position of body parts and the strength of effort used in movement, contributed to significantly by propriospinal fibers, muscle spindles, and Golgi tendon organs. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Are signals from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs mainly processed consciously or subconsciously?","choices":["Mainly consciously, requiring deliberate attention, since they bypass the cerebellum entirely and project only to the cortex","Equally consciously and subconsciously, with no particular bias toward either the cerebellum or intrinsic motor control","Only muscle spindle signals are processed subconsciously, while Golgi tendon organ signals require conscious attention","Mainly subconsciously, for intrinsic muscle control, though they also transmit information to the cerebrum and cerebellum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Signals from muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ sensory receptors are used mainly for intrinsic (subconscious) muscle control, occurring mainly subconsciously, though they also transmit information to the cerebrum and cerebellum. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where are motor neurons located in the spinal cord, and how do they compare in size to other neurons?","choices":["They are scattered diffusely throughout the gray matter and are the same size as interneurons","They are located in the anterior portion of the cord and are 50-100% larger than other neurons","They are located in the lateral horn only and are about 25% smaller than sensory neurons","They are located in the posterior (dorsal) horn and are 50% smaller than sensory neurons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motor neurons are located in the anterior portion of the spinal cord and are 50-100% larger than other neurons, giving rise to nerve fibers that leave the cord via the anterior roots. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Once sensory fibers enter the spinal cord, what two general things can happen to their signal?","choices":["They are transmitted to higher centers, or they synapse locally to elicit motor reflexes","They travel exclusively to the thalamus and never synapse within the spinal cord itself","They are immediately degraded by local peptidases before reaching any synapse","They always ascend directly to the cerebral cortex without any local processing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sensory fibers entering the spinal cord are either transmitted onward to higher centers, or synapse locally within the cord to elicit motor reflexes — illustrating the cord's dual role in relay and local reflex processing. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the three subareas of the motor cortex?","choices":["Broca's area, Wernicke's area, and the angular gyrus","Primary motor cortex, premotor area, and supplemental motor area","Only the primary motor cortex -- subareas exist solely in sensory, not motor, cortex","Occipital, parietal, and cerebellar motor coordination areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"The motor cortex is divided into the primary motor cortex (fine motor movement, unequal topographic representation), the premotor area (movement of muscle groups for a task), and the supplemental motor area (bilateral movements, positional/attitudinal support for fine control). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What deficit results from damage to Broca's area?","choices":["Loss of equilibrium and postural balance control","Complete loss of visual field perception","Decreased speech capability (motor aphasia)","Selective motor apraxia affecting the hands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Damage to Broca's area causes decreased speech capability; a closely associated area controls the appropriate respiratory function needed for speech. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What deficit results from damage to the hand skills area of the motor cortex?","choices":["Complete paralysis of the entire contralateral side of the body","Selective loss of the patellar tendon reflex","Complete loss of auditory perception bilaterally","Motor apraxia — the inability to perform fine hand movements"],"answer":3,"exp":"Damage to the hand skills area of the motor cortex causes motor apraxia, the inability to perform fine hand movements. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"From what three cortical regions does the corticospinal tract originate, and in what approximate proportions?","choices":["Occipital cortex (30%), temporal cortex (30%), and parietal association cortex (40%)","Primary motor cortex (30%), supplementary motor areas (30%), and somatic sensory areas (40%)","Entirely from the primary motor cortex, contributing 100% of corticospinal fibers","Entirely from the cerebellum (100%), with no contribution from the cerebral cortex"],"answer":1,"exp":"The corticospinal tract originates from the primary motor cortex (about 30%), supplementary motor areas (about 30%), and somatic sensory areas (about 40%). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are Betz cells, and what fraction of corticospinal fibers do they contribute?","choices":["Giant pyramidal cells that give rise to large, fast-conducting fibers (~70 m/sec), but make up only about 3% of total corticospinal fibers","Small interneurons found within the anterior horn of the spinal cord that make up the majority of corticospinal fibers, conducting signals at only about 15 m/sec","Glial cells that support and myelinate the corticospinal tract, comprising the majority of the tract's total cross-sectional area within the medulla","Sensory relay neurons found only within the thalamus, projecting exclusively to somatosensory rather than motor cortex and contributing no corticospinal fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Betz cells are giant pyramidal cells in the primary motor cortex that give rise to large corticospinal fibers with fast transmission rates (~70 m/sec), but the roughly 34,000 Betz cell fibers make up only about 3% of the total ~1 million corticospinal fibers — the remaining 97% are small-diameter fibers conducting background tonic signals. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Where do most corticospinal fibers cross to the opposite side of the body?","choices":["In the spinal cord at each segmental level, just before exiting to synapse with anterior motor neurons","In the thalamus, prior to descending together in a single uncrossed corticospinal tract","They never cross to the opposite side, descending the entire way in an uncrossed ipsilateral corticospinal tract","In the medulla, descending afterward in the lateral corticospinal tracts"],"answer":3,"exp":"The majority of corticospinal fibers cross to the opposite side in the medulla and then descend in the lateral corticospinal tracts, synapsing with interneurons, anterior motor neurons, and a few sensory relay neurons in the cord's gray matter. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the difference between a receptor agonist and a receptor antagonist?","choices":["An agonist blocks a biological response by binding to and blocking the receptor; an antagonist initiates a physiological response upon combining with its receptor","They are synonymous terms both describing any substance that binds a receptor and always produces the identical physiological response upon binding","Agonists and antagonists both always increase receptor activity, differing only in how strongly they bind and how long the response lasts","An agonist initiates a physiological response when it combines with its receptor; an antagonist blocks a biological response by binding to and blocking the receptor"],"answer":3,"exp":"A receptor agonist is a substance that initiates a physiological response when it combines with its receptor; a receptor antagonist is a substance that blocks a biological response by binding to and blocking the receptor. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which effector tissues are exceptions to the rule that postganglionic sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine?","choices":["The heart and lungs, whose postganglionic sympathetic fibers release dopamine instead of norepinephrine","Only the adrenal medulla, whose chromaffin cells release acetylcholine directly into the bloodstream instead of norepinephrine","There are no exceptions -- every postganglionic sympathetic fiber releases norepinephrine onto its effector tissue without exception","Sweat glands, piloerector muscles, and select blood vessels (which are cholinergic)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Almost all postganglionic sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine, except for those innervating sweat glands, piloerector muscles, and select blood vessels, which release acetylcholine instead (sympathetic-cholinergic fibers). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What class of drug are phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine, and what do they block?","choices":["Ganglionic blockers, blocking autonomic ganglia","Alpha blockers, blocking alpha receptors","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Beta blockers, blocking beta receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Phentolamine and phenoxybenzamine are alpha blockers, blocking alpha adrenergic receptors, in contrast to beta blockers like propranolol (blocks beta-1 and beta-2) and metoprolol (blocks beta-1 selectively). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the key difference between propranolol and metoprolol as beta blockers?","choices":["Metoprolol blocks both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors non-selectively; propranolol selectively blocks only beta-1 receptors","Neither drug blocks any adrenergic receptor; both act instead by depleting presynaptic norepinephrine storage vesicles","Both drugs have identical non-selective activity, blocking beta-1 and beta-2 receptors equally with no clinical distinction","Propranolol blocks both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors; metoprolol selectively blocks beta-1 receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Propranolol is a non-selective beta blocker (blocking both beta-1 and beta-2 receptors), while metoprolol selectively blocks beta-1 receptors. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How do drugs like ephedrine, tyramine, and amphetamine act on the sympathetic nervous system?","choices":["They permanently destroy norepinephrine storage vesicles in the presynaptic terminal, depleting the neuron's capacity to release transmitter over time","They act indirectly by increasing the release of norepinephrine from its storage terminals","They have no effect on the sympathetic nervous system, acting instead exclusively as reuptake inhibitors at serotonergic synapses in the CNS","They act directly by binding and activating alpha and beta receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, without involving norepinephrine release at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ephedrine, tyramine, and amphetamine act indirectly on the sympathetic nervous system by increasing the release of norepinephrine from its storage terminals, rather than directly binding adrenergic receptors themselves. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How do reserpine and guanethidine differ in their mechanism of blocking sympathetic effects?","choices":["Reserpine blocks synthesis and storage of norepinephrine; guanethidine blocks its release from the nerve terminal","Neither drug affects norepinephrine directly; both instead work by degrading acetylcholine at the postganglionic synapse","Reserpine and guanethidine are both ganglionic blockers that prevent transmission between pre- and postganglionic sympathetic neurons","Both drugs block postsynaptic alpha receptors directly, preventing norepinephrine from binding regardless of how much is released"],"answer":0,"exp":"Reserpine blocks the synthesis and storage of norepinephrine, while guanethidine blocks its release from the nerve terminal — both reduce sympathetic effects but through different steps in the norepinephrine life cycle. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens to cortical function if the thalamic connection to a cortical area is lost, and which sensory pathway is the exception to passing through the thalamus?","choices":["All sensory pathways pass through the thalamus with no exceptions whatsoever in the body","Cortical function increases; the visual pathway is the sole exception, bypassing the thalamus","Cortical function is entirely unaffected; the auditory pathway is the exception, bypassing the thalamus","Cortical function stops; the olfactory tract is the exception (does not pass through the thalamus)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each area of the cortex is connected to a specific part of the thalamus, and when that thalamic connection is lost, cortical function stops; all sensory pathways pass through the thalamus except the olfactory tract. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What percentage of the population has a left-dominant hemisphere, and what is Wernicke's area responsible for in the dominant hemisphere?","choices":["95%; Wernicke's area (up to 50% larger in the dominant hemisphere) is responsible for verbal symbolism and related intelligence","100%; Wernicke's area has no specific known function in either cerebral hemisphere at all","5%; Wernicke's area instead controls only music perception in the nondominant hemisphere","50%; Wernicke's area instead controls motor speech coordination rather than comprehension"],"answer":0,"exp":"95% of the population has a left-dominant hemisphere, where Wernicke's area (which can be up to 50% larger than in the nondominant hemisphere) is responsible for verbal symbolism and related intelligence. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What type of intelligence is the nondominant hemisphere more associated with?","choices":["Other forms of sensory intelligence, such as music and sensory feelings","Only autonomic regulation of heart rate and digestion","Verbal symbolism and language comprehension abilities","Only fine motor coordination of the hands"],"answer":0,"exp":"While the dominant hemisphere (usually left) is specialized for verbal symbolism via Wernicke's area, the nondominant hemisphere is related to other forms of sensory intelligence, such as music and sensory feelings. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What deficit results from damage to the prefrontal association area?","choices":["Complete loss of both long-term and short-term memory function entirely","Inability to keep track of simultaneous bits of information and being easily distracted, impairing concerted logical thinking","Loss of the patellar reflex and all other spinal cord stretch reflexes","Loss of vision only, with every other cognitive function fully preserved"],"answer":1,"exp":"The prefrontal association area is responsible for calling forth stored information toward a goal and concerted logical thinking; damage causes an inability to keep track of simultaneous bits of information and easy distractibility. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In the pathway for auditory communication (hearing a word and responding), what happens after the primary auditory area recognizes a sound as a word and Wernicke's area interprets it?","choices":["The word/thought is formed, transmitted via the arcuate fasciculus to Broca's area, which activates motor programs for word formation, then signals are sent to the motor cortex to control speech muscles","Nothing further happens at all — comprehension in Wernicke's area, without any further transmission elsewhere, is considered the complete and final step in the entire pathway","The signal is transmitted directly from the primary auditory area to the cerebellum via the inferior peduncle, where it is stored permanently as a long-term motor memory trace","The signal is immediately sent from the primary auditory area to the spinal cord directly, bypassing the arcuate fasciculus, Broca's area, and the motor cortex entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"After the primary auditory area recognizes a sound as a word and Wernicke's area interprets its meaning, the word that expresses the thought is formed, transmitted via the arcuate fasciculus to Broca's area, which activates motor programs for word formation, and finally signals are sent to the motor cortex to control the speech muscles. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the functional consequence of destroying the visual and auditory association areas (Wernicke aphasia)?","choices":["Loss of motor speech coordination only, with comprehension fully intact","An inability to understand the written or spoken word","Complete paralysis of the muscles used for speech","Loss of all long-term declarative memory function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Wernicke aphasia results from destruction of the visual and auditory association areas and causes an inability to understand the written or spoken word — a receptive/comprehension deficit, distinct from Broca's area's motor speech deficit. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
